5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. To make a word appear more prestigious.

The French accent circonflexe is an etymological / historical marker, not a stylistic one. The functions that are documented for it are

* B – homophone distinction, e.g. du / dû, sur / sûr, ou / où, etc.;
* D – the Old French length marker, the circumflex is the reflex of a historically long vowel;
* H – the classic s-deletion marker, e.g. forêt < forest, hôte < hoste, bête < bestia, etc., and more generally the loss of a sibilant or of t, d, etc., that was once written;
* G / I – the same loss of a consonant between vowels is the historical source of the monophthongisation / hiatus resolution that is written with a circumflex, e.g. â, ê, î, ô, û from former diphthongs / hiatus;
* A – in present day French the circumflex is the conventional way of writing the historically long, i.e. phonetically open, quality of the vowel, â ~ [ɑ] vs a ~ [a], ê ~ [ɛ] etc.; and
* C is the particular Latin reflex of that length system, the ô-class etc.

None of the historical grammars of French orthography record an orthographic use of the circumflex as a marker of register or prestige. The “prestige” effect is a modern sociolinguistic perception of the archaic spelling, it is never a codified function of the sign.

