2.text:
F. To make a word appear more prestigious.

The accent circonflexe is an etymological / diachronic sign. In the historical and normative literature it is regularly described as

* B the classic homophone disambiguator, du / dû, sur / sûr, mur / mûr, etc.;
* H the compensatory-lengthening / loss marker, most famously the loss of a post-vocalic sibilant that was written and pronounced, forêt < forest, hôte < hoste, bête < bestia, etc.;
* G/I the reflex of an Old French diphthong / hiatus that has been monophthongised / resolved, â < au, ô < ou/au, etc.;
* D the reflex in Old French orthography of the Latin accentus circumflexus / the Old French long vowel notation ā ē ī ō ū < Latin long vowels. The length is no longer phonemic in modern French but the marking is historically attested.

The modern open-quality effect routinely taught as ê [ɛ] vs é [e], â [ɑ] vs a, etc., A, and the particular Latin reflex reading C are descriptions of the outcome of the length / loss system, and the s-deletion and diphthongal reflexes are the normal sources of the homophone disambiguation B.

A register / prestige / aesthetic motive for the sign is a modern sociolinguistic perception of the archaism of the circumflex and the resistance to the 1990 simplifications. No historical grammar of French orthography records a codified orthographic function of the circumflex as a marker of register or prestige. It is a folk commentary on the sign, not an attested formal function.

